              "instructions": "Read the passage, then read again and choose the correct answers. Questions 39-42: /4x1=4 points/; questions 43-44: /2x3=6 points/",
              "text": "(1) Every day, people around the world drink more than two billion cups of coffee. For many, it is the first thing they reach for in the morning. Yet few coffee drinkers know much about the long journey their drink has made, from a hillside farm to their cup.\n\n(2) Coffee comes from the seeds of a plant that grows best in tropical regions near the equator. The seeds, usually called beans, grow inside small red fruits known as cherries. Much of the world's coffee is picked by hand, because the cherries on a single plant do not all ripen at the same time.\n\n(3) After picking, the beans are removed from the fruit and dried, often in the sun. They are then shipped green to other countries, where they are roasted. Roasting gives coffee its brown colour and most of its flavour; the same beans can taste very different depending on how long they are roasted.\n\n(4) Coffee is popular not only for its taste but also for its effect. It contains caffeine, which helps people feel more awake and concentrate better. Many studies suggest that moderate amounts are safe for most adults. However, too much caffeine can cause headaches, a fast heartbeat and difficulty sleeping.\n\n(5) The coffee trade also raises questions of fairness. Millions of small farmers grow coffee, but the price they receive is often only a small part of what customers pay in a café. Some organisations now certify coffee as \"fair trade\", promising that farmers receive a minimum price.\n\n(6) So, next time you drink a cup of coffee, think about the people and the process behind it. A little knowledge, perhaps, can make the drink taste even better.",
